# 01 – Reconnaissance

> **OWASP Juice Shop Web Application VAPT – Reconnaissance Phase**

Reconnaissance is the first phase of this assessment. The objective is to understand the Juice Shop application's **technology stack, attack surface, routes, API endpoints, parameters, authentication boundaries, client-side assets, HTTP behavior, and exposed information** before performing vulnerability testing.

The reconnaissance phase follows:

```text
Target Identification
        ↓
Technology Fingerprinting
        ↓
HTTP Enumeration
        ↓
Application Crawling
        ↓
Endpoint / API Discovery
        ↓
JavaScript Analysis
        ↓
Parameter Identification
        ↓
Authentication Mapping
        ↓
Attack-Surface Mapping
        ↓
Evidence Collection
```

---

# 🎯 Objectives

The objectives of this phase are:

* Identify the target application.
* Identify the target host and port.
* Identify the application technology stack.
* Identify frontend and backend technologies.
* Identify HTTP methods.
* Enumerate application routes.
* Discover API endpoints.
* Identify interesting parameters.
* Identify authentication-related endpoints.
* Identify cookies and session mechanisms.
* Analyze JavaScript files.
* Review `robots.txt`, sitemap and other public resources.
* Identify exposed files and directories.
* Identify interesting HTTP headers.
* Build an attack-surface inventory.
* Record evidence before exploitation.

---

# ⚠️ Scope & Authorization

This assessment is performed against a **local/authorized OWASP Juice Shop laboratory environment**.

Example target:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

If Juice Shop is running on another host:

```text
http://<TARGET-IP>:3000
```

Replace `<TARGET-IP>` with the IP address of your authorized laboratory target.

Do not execute these techniques against systems without explicit authorization.

---

# 🧪 Lab Setup

## Start Juice Shop

If Juice Shop is installed locally:

```bash
npm start
```

Or:

```bash
npm run serve
```

If using Docker:

```bash
docker run --rm -p 3000:3000 bkimminich/juice-shop
```

Verify:

```bash
curl -I http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

Expected result:

```text
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
```

Open:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

---

# 1. Target Identification

Define the target before beginning testing.

```bash
TARGET="http://127.0.0.1:3000"
```

Check connectivity:

```bash
curl -I "$TARGET"
```

Check response:

```bash
curl -s "$TARGET" | head
```

Check HTTP status:

```bash
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" "$TARGET"
```

Expected:

```text
200
```

---

# 2. Host & Port Enumeration

If the target is running on another authorized lab machine:

```bash
nmap -Pn <TARGET-IP>
```

Focused web-port scan:

```bash
nmap -Pn -p 80,443,3000,8000,8080,8443 <TARGET-IP>
```

Service/version detection:

```bash
nmap -Pn -sV -p 3000 <TARGET-IP>
```

More detailed enumeration:

```bash
nmap -Pn -sC -sV -p 3000 <TARGET-IP>
```

### Example

```text
PORT     STATE SERVICE VERSION
3000/tcp open  http    Node.js Express
```

> Record only ports relevant to the authorized Juice Shop environment.

---

# 3. Technology Fingerprinting

## WhatWeb

Run:

```bash
whatweb "$TARGET"
```

Example:

```bash
whatweb http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

Look for:

* Angular
* Node.js
* Express
* JavaScript libraries
* Web server information
* Cookies
* Framework indicators

---

## Nmap HTTP Enumeration

```bash
nmap -Pn -p 3000 --script http-title,http-headers,http-methods <TARGET-IP>
```

This can provide information about:

* HTTP title
* Headers
* Supported methods

---

# 4. HTTP Response Analysis

Inspect the complete response:

```bash
curl -i "$TARGET"
```

Inspect only headers:

```bash
curl -I "$TARGET"
```

Save headers:

```bash
curl -I "$TARGET" > evidence-http-headers.txt
```

Inspect server information:

```bash
curl -s -D - -o /dev/null "$TARGET"
```

Check supported HTTP methods:

```bash
curl -i -X OPTIONS "$TARGET"
```

Record:

```text
HTTP Version:
Status Code:
Server:
Content-Type:
Set-Cookie:
CORS:
CSP:
HSTS:
X-Content-Type-Options:
X-Frame-Options:
Referrer-Policy:
```

---

# 5. Browser-Based Reconnaissance

Open:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

Open browser Developer Tools:

```text
F12
```

Important tabs:

```text
Elements
Console
Network
Sources
Application
Security
```

---

# 6. Application Mapping

Before actively testing vulnerabilities, browse the application normally.

Visit:

```text
/
```

Then identify:

* Homepage
* Login
* Registration
* Products
* Search
* Basket
* Checkout
* Orders
* Customer feedback
* Complaints
* User profile
* Administration-related functionality
* Search functionality
* File upload functionality
* Reviews
* Wallet/payment-related functionality

Record discovered functionality in:

```text
Application Functionality
```

---

# 7. Burp Suite Setup

## Step 1 – Start Burp Suite

Open:

```text
Burp Suite
```

Go to:

```text
Proxy → Proxy settings
```

Default proxy:

```text
127.0.0.1:8080
```

Configure the browser to use:

```text
HTTP Proxy:
127.0.0.1

Port:
8080
```

---

# 8. Burp Proxy Workflow

Recommended workflow:

```text
Browser
   │
   ▼
Burp Proxy
   │
   ├── HTTP History
   │
   ├── Repeater
   │
   ├── Site Map
   │
   └── Request/Response
           │
           ▼
      Juice Shop
```

---

# 9. Capture Initial Request

Open the Juice Shop homepage while Burp Proxy is running.

Go to:

```text
Proxy → HTTP history
```

Record:

```text
Method
Host
Port
Path
Parameters
Cookies
Headers
Status Code
Response Length
Content-Type
```

Example:

```http
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: 127.0.0.1:3000
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0
Accept: text/html
```

---

# 10. Build Burp Site Map

Go to:

```text
Target → Site map
```

Browse the application normally.

Burp should begin discovering:

```text
/
├── assets/
├── api/
├── rest/
├── administration/
├── login
├── register
├── basket
├── search
├── profile
└── other application routes
```

> The exact routes observed should be taken from your current Juice Shop instance rather than blindly copying a list from another version.

---

# 11. Endpoint Discovery

Endpoint discovery should use multiple sources.

```text
Browser
   +
Burp HTTP History
   +
Burp Site Map
   +
JavaScript
   +
robots.txt
   +
sitemap
   +
Application behavior
```

---

# 12. robots.txt

Request:

```bash
curl -i "$TARGET/robots.txt"
```

Or:

```bash
curl "$TARGET/robots.txt"
```

Save:

```bash
curl "$TARGET/robots.txt" > robots.txt
```

Review for:

```text
Disallowed paths
Hidden routes
Application directories
Administrative paths
```

---

# 13. sitemap.xml

Check:

```bash
curl -i "$TARGET/sitemap.xml"
```

If present:

```bash
curl "$TARGET/sitemap.xml" > sitemap.xml
```

Review discovered paths.

---

# 14. security.txt

Check:

```bash
curl -i "$TARGET/.well-known/security.txt"
```

Also:

```bash
curl -i "$TARGET/security.txt"
```

Record whether the file exists and what information it exposes.

---

# 15. Favicon Fingerprinting

Download favicon:

```bash
wget "$TARGET/favicon.ico"
```

Check file:

```bash
file favicon.ico
```

Generate hash:

```bash
md5sum favicon.ico
```

This can help identify application fingerprints when comparing environments.

---

# 16. JavaScript Reconnaissance

Modern web applications frequently expose useful application information through JavaScript bundles.

Open:

```text
Browser → Developer Tools → Sources
```

Look for:

```text
main.js
runtime.js
polyfills.js
vendor.js
chunk files
```

---

# 17. Extract JavaScript Files

Using Burp:

```text
Target → Site map
```

Filter:

```text
.js
```

Export or inspect JavaScript files.

Using command-line tools:

```bash
curl -s "$TARGET" | grep -oE 'src="[^"]+\.js[^"]*"'
```

Extract JavaScript URLs:

```bash
curl -s "$TARGET" | grep -oE 'src="[^"]+\.js[^"]*"' | sed 's/src="//;s/"$//'
```

---

# 18. Search JavaScript for Endpoints

Once JavaScript files are downloaded:

```bash
grep -RniE 'api/|rest/|graphql|login|register|basket|orders|users|products' .
```

Search for URL-like strings:

```bash
grep -RnoE '["'\''][^"'\'']*/(api|rest)/[^"'\'']*["'\'']' .
```

Search for potentially interesting keywords:

```bash
grep -RniE 'admin|authenticate|authorization|token|password|user|basket|order|upload|feedback' .
```

> JavaScript findings are reconnaissance leads. They should be verified against actual application requests before being treated as active endpoints.

---

# 19. API Discovery Through Burp

This is one of the most important parts of the Juice Shop reconnaissance phase.

Browse:

```text
Products
Search
Login
Register
Basket
Profile
Orders
Reviews
Feedback
```

Then inspect:

```text
Proxy → HTTP history
```

Filter:

```text
api
```

Also inspect:

```text
rest
```

Record every relevant request.

---

# 20. API Inventory

Create a table such as:

| Method | Endpoint    | Parameters | Auth   | Purpose            | Evidence   |
| ------ | ----------- | ---------- | ------ | ------------------ | ---------- |
| GET    | `/api/...`  | `id`       | No/Yes | Product data       | Screenshot |
| POST   | `/rest/...` | JSON body  | Yes    | Application action | Screenshot |
| GET    | `/api/...`  | `q`        | No     | Search             | Screenshot |

Do not mark a vulnerability at this stage.

This table is an **attack-surface inventory**.

---

# 21. API Request Analysis

For each API request, inspect:

### Request

```text
HTTP method
URL
Query parameters
Headers
Cookies
Authorization
Content-Type
JSON body
```

### Response

```text
Status code
Content-Type
Response body
Returned objects
User information
Error messages
Server headers
```

Example:

```http
POST /api/example HTTP/1.1
Host: 127.0.0.1:3000
Content-Type: application/json
Cookie: ...

{
    "example": "value"
}
```

---

# 22. Parameter Discovery

Identify:

### URL parameters

```text
?id=
?search=
?q=
?item=
?quantity=
```

### JSON parameters

```json
{
  "email": "...",
  "password": "...",
  "id": "...",
  "quantity": "..."
}
```

### Form parameters

```text
username
password
email
message
comment
```

### Headers

```text
Authorization
Origin
Referer
Content-Type
Accept
Cookie
User-Agent
```

Record these for later vulnerability testing.

---

# 23. Authentication Surface Mapping

Identify authentication-related requests.

Typical functionality:

```text
Login
Registration
Password reset
Logout
Profile
Session
Authentication token
```

Use Burp:

```text
Proxy → HTTP history
```

Filter for:

```text
login
register
authenticate
logout
password
token
```

Record:

```text
Login endpoint
HTTP method
Username/email parameter
Password parameter
Authentication token
Cookie
Session behavior
Response code
```

---

# 24. Cookie Reconnaissance

Inspect:

```http
Set-Cookie:
```

Example fields:

```text
Name
Value
Secure
HttpOnly
SameSite
Path
Domain
Expires
```

Use:

```bash
curl -I "$TARGET"
```

Look specifically for:

```text
Set-Cookie
```

Document cookie security separately during the HTTP Security phase.

---

# 25. CORS Reconnaissance

Inspect:

```text
Access-Control-Allow-Origin
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials
Access-Control-Allow-Methods
Access-Control-Allow-Headers
```

Check baseline:

```bash
curl -i "$TARGET"
```

For an authorized lab test, inspect behavior with an Origin header:

```bash
curl -i \
-H "Origin: https://example.com" \
"$TARGET"
```

Record whether the application returns CORS-related headers.

> This is reconnaissance. Do not conclude that CORS is vulnerable solely because a header exists.

---

# 26. HTTP Method Enumeration

Check:

```bash
curl -i -X OPTIONS "$TARGET"
```

Review:

```text
Allow:
Access-Control-Allow-Methods:
```

Also observe methods naturally used by the application:

```text
GET
POST
PUT
PATCH
DELETE
OPTIONS
```

The purpose is to understand the application's HTTP attack surface.

---

# 27. Content-Type Enumeration

Record content types used by endpoints.

Common examples:

```text
application/json
text/html
application/javascript
application/x-www-form-urlencoded
multipart/form-data
```

This is useful for later:

* API testing
* Input validation
* XSS testing
* Request smuggling research
* HTTP security testing

---

# 28. Interesting Files & Paths

Check common application resources:

```bash
for path in \
robots.txt \
sitemap.xml \
security.txt \
.well-known/security.txt \
favicon.ico \
manifest.json; do
    echo "===== $path ====="
    curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} %{size_download}\n" "$TARGET/$path"
done
```

Record only resources actually exposed by the target.

---

# 29. Content Discovery

For an authorized lab environment, `ffuf` can be used to discover content.

Example:

```bash
ffuf \
-u "$TARGET/FUZZ" \
-w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt \
-mc 200,204,301,302,307,401,403
```

For JavaScript-related discovery:

```bash
ffuf \
-u "$TARGET/FUZZ" \
-w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt \
-e .js,.json,.txt
```

Important:

> Do not assume every discovered path is a vulnerability. Discovery and vulnerability validation are separate activities.

---

# 30. Nuclei – Passive/Controlled Recon

Nuclei may be used for controlled technology and exposure checks:

```bash
nuclei -u "$TARGET"
```

For a limited template category:

```bash
nuclei -u "$TARGET" -tags tech
```

Record scanner output separately.

Automated results must be manually verified before reporting a finding.

---

# 31. curl Recon Cheat Sheet

### Headers

```bash
curl -I "$TARGET"
```

### Full response

```bash
curl -i "$TARGET"
```

### Follow redirects

```bash
curl -i -L "$TARGET"
```

### OPTIONS

```bash
curl -i -X OPTIONS "$TARGET"
```

### Save response

```bash
curl -s "$TARGET" -o response.html
```

### Status code

```bash
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" "$TARGET"
```

### Response size

```bash
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{size_download}\n" "$TARGET"
```

### Timing

```bash
curl -s -o /dev/null \
-w "Status: %{http_code}\nTime: %{time_total}s\n" \
"$TARGET"
```

---

# 32. Burp Repeater Workflow

Once an interesting request is identified:

```text
HTTP History
      ↓
Right Click
      ↓
Send to Repeater
      ↓
Modify Request
      ↓
Send
      ↓
Compare Response
      ↓
Record Observation
```

During reconnaissance, use Repeater primarily to understand:

* Required parameters
* Optional parameters
* Response behavior
* Authentication requirements
* Status codes
* Object identifiers
* API structure

Do not immediately attempt destructive payloads.

---

# 33. Attack-Surface Mapping

Create an attack-surface map.

```text
                     Juice Shop
                         │
        ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
        │                │                │
     Frontend           APIs          Authentication
        │                │                │
   Angular Routes    REST/API        Login/Register
        │                │                │
   JS Bundles        Parameters      Sessions/Tokens
        │                │                │
        └────────────────┼────────────────┘
                         │
                  Security Testing
                         │
       ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
       │                 │                 │
   Injection            XSS              Access
       │                 │              Control
       │                 │                 │
     SQLi              Stored/DOM        IDOR/BOLA
```

---

# 34. Reconnaissance Findings Table

Use this table throughout the assessment.

| ID    | Observation                        | Type              | Severity | Evidence   | Status    |
| ----- | ---------------------------------- | ----------------- | -------- | ---------- | --------- |
| R-001 | Application exposed on HTTP port   | Attack Surface    | Info     | Screenshot | Confirmed |
| R-002 | Technology stack identified        | Fingerprinting    | Info     | WhatWeb    | Confirmed |
| R-003 | API endpoint discovered            | Endpoint          | Info     | Burp       | Confirmed |
| R-004 | Authentication endpoint identified | Attack Surface    | Info     | Burp       | Confirmed |
| R-005 | JavaScript bundle discovered       | Client-side Asset | Info     | Browser    | Confirmed |
| R-006 | Interesting parameter identified   | Input Surface     | Info     | Burp       | Confirmed |
| R-007 | Security-related header observed   | HTTP              | Info     | Response   | Confirmed |
| R-008 | Public resource discovered         | Information       | Info     | curl       | Confirmed |

> Reconnaissance observations should not automatically be classified as vulnerabilities.

---

# 35. Endpoint Inventory

Maintain a separate endpoint inventory.

| ID    | Method | Endpoint | Auth | Parameter | Content-Type     | Purpose            | Tested |
| ----- | ------ | -------- | ---- | --------- | ---------------- | ------------------ | ------ |
| E-001 | GET    | `/`      | No   | —         | text/html        | Homepage           | ✓      |
| E-002 | GET    | `/...`   | No   | `id`      | JSON             | Data retrieval     | ✓      |
| E-003 | POST   | `/...`   | Yes  | JSON      | application/json | Application action | ✓      |
| E-004 | GET    | `/...`   | Yes  | —         | JSON             | User data          | ✓      |

Replace the placeholder endpoints with the actual requests captured from your Juice Shop version.

---

# 36. Parameter Inventory

| ID    | Endpoint | Parameter  | Location | Data Type | Authentication | Future Test    |
| ----- | -------- | ---------- | -------- | --------- | -------------- | -------------- |
| P-001 | `/...`   | `id`       | Query    | Integer   | No             | Access Control |
| P-002 | `/...`   | `search`   | Query    | String    | No             | Injection/XSS  |
| P-003 | `/...`   | `email`    | JSON     | String    | Yes            | Authentication |
| P-004 | `/...`   | `quantity` | JSON     | Integer   | Yes            | Business Logic |

---

# 37. Authentication Boundary Map

Document which functions require authentication.

```text
Public
│
├── Homepage
├── Product browsing
├── Search
└── Registration
        │
        ▼
Authenticated User
│
├── Basket
├── Profile
├── Orders
├── Reviews
└── Account functionality
        │
        ▼
Potential Privileged Functions
│
└── Administrative functionality
```

The exact access boundaries should be verified from your current test environment.

